WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
(upbeat music)

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.400
- [Narrator] Ever think
of how many breaths of air

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.910
you take in a day?

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.080
It's a lot, like 20 thousand,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:09.390
give or take a few.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.130
All day, all night, our bodies are at work

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.680
bringing in the good (bell dings)

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.720
and kicking out the bad (buzzer sounds)

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.910
But as important as this
is to our very existence,

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.410
there's still a lot that
many of us misunderstand

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:22.960
about what's really in our air.

00:00:25.441 --> 00:00:28.024
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:33.940
- Most people don't know very
much about air pollution.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.160
We don't sort of, see
this invisible connection

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.203
between that pollution and
very real health problems.

00:00:42.112 --> 00:00:43.010
(man coughing)

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.600
I'm Beth Gardiner, I'm an
environmental journalist

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.140
and author of the book
"Choked, Life and Breath

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.760
in the Age of Air Pollution."

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.860
(pages flipping)
(bell dings)

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.080
Air pollution is killing

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.520
millions of people around the world.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:58.730
The World Health Organization estimates

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.750
seven million annually,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.475
and causing a long list of illnesses.

00:01:04.475 --> 00:01:06.408
(keyboard clicking)

00:01:06.408 --> 00:01:09.620
(somber music)

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.220
Air pollution affects almost every

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.850
biological system in our bodies.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.740
Increased rates of air pollution

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.750
are linked to higher
rates of heart attacks,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:23.450
of strokes, of premature
birth, of multiple different

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.780
kinds of cancer, of Alzheimer's disease,

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.770
and cognitive problems in kids, diabetes

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:33.680
and obesity and the list just
goes on and on and on and on.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.040
The pollutant that scientists
have really honed in on

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.072
in recent years as the most worrying

00:01:40.072 --> 00:01:42.440
is called particulate pollution.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.010
- [Scientist] If this man
is continuously exposed

00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:47.700
to this sort of atmosphere,
then there will be

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.653
a buildup of these particles.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.620
- It's tiny particles called PM2.5.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.520
They are less than 2.5 microns in diameter

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.820
that is about a 30th of
the width of a human hair.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.170
PM2.5 and even smaller ones,
which are called ultra fine

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.380
are so small that they
can actually penetrate

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.490
deep down into the lungs,

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.560
all the way into the tiny
little sacs called alveoli,

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.160
Where the air within our lungs

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.040
crosses into the bloodstream.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.710
Once they're in the bloodstream,

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.450
they can travel all around the body

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.573
and impact all of our different organs.

00:02:27.635 --> 00:02:29.590
(keyboard clicking)

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.090
A lot of people really
associate air pollution

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.780
with cities, that this
is an urban problem.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:36.480
Air doesn't know any boundaries,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.800
it doesn't stop at the city limits.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.523
These tiny particles can actually
travel thousands of miles.

00:02:43.569 --> 00:02:45.070
There are a lot of different pollutants

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.150
mostly related to burning things.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:51.623
Oil and gas and diesel and
wood and coal and garbage.

00:02:52.912 --> 00:02:54.750
(mellow country music)

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.400
Agriculture is another, unexpected,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.340
but pretty significant
contributor to air pollution.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:02.260
The different chemicals that come off of

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.070
fertilizers and manure combine in the air

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.097
with other pollutants.

00:03:06.097 --> 00:03:07.680
- [Vintage Cartoon Narrator] And the smell

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.190
can be a real nuisance to your neighbors.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.690
So it's a lot of different
things in the air.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:14.930
Whether you're living
close to a factory farm

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.225
or hundreds of miles away from them.

00:03:17.225 --> 00:03:19.120
(birds singing)

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.750
But this is not a problem
that is equally distributed.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.580
It's not just cities,

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.060
it's where you're going to end up living

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:28.580
if you have lower housing costs.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.410
The neighborhoods that
are most heavily impacted

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.530
very often are poorer,
people of color, immigrants,

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:38.960
people who lack the ability
to move somewhere cleaner.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.790
Those tend to be the places where

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.290
these really polluting
facilities get sited.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.880
So this is really a problem
that tracks very much

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:50.890
with the fault lines
of economic inequality

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:52.193
and racial injustice.

00:03:53.853 --> 00:03:55.103
(keyboard clicking)

00:03:55.103 --> 00:03:57.350
(happy music)

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:00.240
When we talk about
environmental challenges

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.830
and how to fix them, the conversation

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:05.902
often gets really, really
focused on what it's gonna cost.

00:04:05.902 --> 00:04:07.200
(cash register dings)

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.050
In terms of public health, productivity,

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.480
longer life spans, the
benefits in dollar terms

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:17.850
of cleaner air are dozens of
times greater than the costs.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.190
But of course, the costs have to paid

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.260
up front by industries.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.260
And the benefits are reaped
years later across society.

00:04:28.233 --> 00:04:30.020
(presidential music)

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.600
The United States has historically been

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.210
an impressive global air
quality success story.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.170
Because of this very strong,

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.530
very innovative law, the clean air act.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.270
Supported, by the way, by both
Democrats and Republicans.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:47.225
That started with getting
lead out of the air.

00:04:47.225 --> 00:04:49.307
Lead was in gasoline.

00:04:49.307 --> 00:04:52.720
There's 70 percent less
pollution in the air

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.940
than there was in 1970
because of this law.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.370
The lesson that comes from that

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.600
is that we know what works.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.030
This is not an impossible problem.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:06.060
Science based regulations

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.073
that prioritize public health works.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.120
All these illnesses and death,

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.500
it can feel sort of overwhelming.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:18.610
But it's also quite inspiring

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.817
when you look at what
has been achieved so far.

00:05:21.817 --> 00:05:23.700
- [Narrator] There are
also innovative solutions

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:26.320
in the works to drastically
reduce pollutants

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.570
before they even get into our air.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.620
Bold ideas that take us
away from burning things,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.260
to cleaner energy.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.720
Re-imagining urban spaces

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:37.150
that are friendlier to
bikers and pedestrians.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:38.720
And shifting to electric vehicles

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.640
to get us everywhere else.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.900
That kind of ingenuity
might be the difference

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.079
between breathing dirty air now

00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:45.380
(man coughs)

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.083
or truly fresh air in the future.

